A||||7||2birds||Cracked EGR Pipe...2.8 V-6, decent junkyard finds though.||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||08-24-2001||02:34 PM||||A new EGR pipe kit is $80 at my local GM dealer.<P>Went junkyarding today in Jacksonville, FL; found two V-6's (an 85 GT and an 86 SE, both automatics), lots of 4 cylinders (one 88).  One EGR pipe was already broken, the other had already been taken.  Two very usable OEM sunroofs if anyone is interested (glass is $15 each).  One V-6 had an engine fire, but the engine is pretty intact, with the exception of the air intake, vacuum lines, and wiring.  I snatched the rear exhaust manifold and y-pipe, heat shields, and alternator bracket from the other (forward manifold was missing already)  Both have all the AC brackets still intact.<P>I checked the EGR pipe from early iron headed cavalier/pontiac 6000's and from F-bodies with 2.8.  They are the same length and shape, but the flange on one end doesnt match and the flanges are welded.<P>If anybody needs any parts, e-mail me for directions to this yard.  They have pretty reasonable prices, but most interior stuff is too far gone.<P>I'm going to try some exhaust repair putty from Pep Boys (good to 2000 degrees), with a hose clamp for support before I drop $80 for a new pipe.||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-24-2001||05:46 PM||||chevy S10 bolts right up. But next time I need to replace, im using the braided stainless one. $90, but last one itll ever need.||||reg||1
Z||000002||samoht||08-24-2001||07:56 PM||||i need an egr tube too, any 2.8 S series truck will work???||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-24-2001||09:58 PM||||I didn't think to check the S-10's because they are throttle body injected, instead of multi-port.  Which years apply?<P>Where did you get the braided stainless EGR tube?  I saw one on a 3800, but it was a threaded connection.<P>So far the repair is holding.  I warmed it up tonight to help cure the patch compound.  I'll drive it tomorrow and see how it does.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 08-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Two88GTs||08-24-2001||11:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>A new EGR pipe kit is $80 at my local GM dealer. I checked the EGR pipe from early iron headed cavalier/pontiac 6000's and from F-bodies with 2.8.  They are the same length and shape, but the flange on one end doesnt match and the flanges are welded.<P>I'm going to try some exhaust repair putty from Pep Boys (good to 2000 degrees), with a hose clamp for support before I drop $80 for a new pipe.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you're willing to use up a Dremel bit or two, you can elongate the bolt holes on the non-matching flange side and get the 2.8L Firebird/Camaro EGR tube to fit(I have one on my 69k 88GT)||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-24-2001||11:47 PM||||I thought out that after I read about it on the Fiero Online Service Guide.  For $3 each, I could have had as many as I wanted.  If the patch stuff doesn't work out, I'lll probably try that route, or the S-10..||||reg||1
